What the source build contains, beyond the release DLL
------------------------------------------------------

1. A, source form -- no second 81-slot ceiling

   The release DLL checked a weapon give twice: the pickup gate (RVA 0x567a0) counted the
   bonus ts_setuserslots stores, then the inventory add routine (RVA 0x622d0) refused past a
   fixed 81 carried-weight. Patch A nop'd the second check. In the source there is no second
   check to remove: CTSGun::TakeWeapon (dlls\ts_gun.cpp) has no weight test at all, and the
   only gate is TSPlayerFreeSlots (dlls\ts_gun.cpp; RVA 0x61460 in the release), which is
   81 - carried - flag(20) - kevlar(10) + the plugin's bonus. A player the plugin never gave
   room is therefore still held to 81 exactly as before.

2. B, source form -- the powerup grant, for ts_matchmode only

   CTSPowerUp::Spawn (dlls\ts_powerup.cpp) arms the powerup's touch handler at spawn, before
   the materialize think, when spawnflags has bit 30 (SF_TSM_PWUP_GRANT) and the cvar
   tsm_enabled exists and is non-zero. Every other powerup still gets Touch = NULL until it
   lands with nopowerup off, so nopowerup 1 still blocks every other powerup, and every server
   without ts_matchmode. This is the source-level replacement for patch B's 7-byte hook and
   its .h9pwup stub.

3. The disarm fix

   A disarm calls CBasePlayer::DropWeapon(-2, damage); past the melee-pressure threshold it
   calls CTSGun::DropWeapon(type, fThrow = false). That used to clear the item and leave the
   player holding a weapon with nothing owned behind it -- still deployed, still firable.
   CTSGun::DropWeapon now calls HolsterWeapon() when the dropped type is the active one and
   fThrow is false, so the player goes to bare hands (viewmodel, weaponmodel, active pointers,
   m_weaponType -> TSW_MELEE, kung-fu draw). A real throw (the drop key, type -1) still calls
   GetBetterWeapon() and auto-selects the next-best weapon, as before.

   Faithful to TS: a normal weapon is removed outright; stackable spares (knife, sealknife,
   m61) and a single whose akimbo is also owned keep the remainder, and those paths already
   holstered.

4. Interface 5/6 alias

   The source defines RETS_INTERFACE_VERSION 6 and appends PowerUpTouch after every v5 slot.
   The deployed reTSX module (tsx_amxx.dll) still calls ReTS_GetInterface(5) and never reads
   the table's version field, so against a source build every ReTS native returned null
   (ts_setusercash, weaponrestriction, ...). dlls\rets_api.cpp now builds two tables:
   BuildInterface(6) for callers that ask for 6, and BuildInterface(5) -- the same 0xD0-byte
   layout with version = 5 -- for callers that ask for 5. Both are static, filled at load.
   Interface 5 is a strict prefix of 6, so a v5 caller reading any v5 slot gets the right
   function; it never reads the appended PowerUpTouch.

5. The two upstream commits of 2026-10-29

   e82e40282a4d "Moving fire penalises only the M60 and the Barrett, as TS 3.0 does"
     dlls\ts_gun.cpp, CTSGun::StandardFire: the x5 moving growth is now the M60's alone
     (over 175 u/s), and the Barrett sets the spread accumulator to 0.3 over 35 u/s. Every
     other gun keeps its standing growth however fast the player moves.

   bd3f6c3c1aa3 "Gunshot and ricochet pitch unclamped, as TS 3.0 plays them"
     cl_dll\ev_ts.cpp: the 255/1 clamp is gone from the gunshot, linked-fire and ricochet
     pitches. pitch = 100 + 300 * slow, 400 at normal speed; neither GoldSrc nor Xash caps
     pitch, so normal fire plays at 4x again instead of 2.55x.


Building and deploying
----------------------

    cd reTS
    $env:RETS_REBUILD = '1'
    .\scripts\build-win32-dropin.ps1

writes binaries\ts\dlls\mp.dll and binaries\ts\cl_dlls\client.dll. xash.bat's assemble step
sets RETS_REBUILD=1 itself, so the H9XASH package takes this pair rather than the older
release_binaries pair. Without RETS_REBUILD=1 the script copies release_binaries\ts\ -- the
shipped release DLL with binary patches A and B (see the appendix) -- and none of the changes
above is present.


